Tier 5 -- adapter conformance, shape B (reaper guest, real CBSD).

One conformance suite run against every adapter, asserting identical
contracts: same fields, same error shapes, same exit-code discipline. The real
side drives an actual jail through its lifecycle. This is the tier that breaks
first when CBSD changes an invocation convention. See TESTING.md §6.

THE SUITE ALREADY EXISTS AND ALREADY RUNS -- against the mock, in tier 4
(tests/tier4/t_conformance_mock.sh). It is:

  tests/conformance/vectors.tsv   function, args, stdout shape, exit status
  tests/conformance/run.subr      the runner: three assertions per vector --
                                  the status, the shape of every line, and
                                  that a function which promised output
                                  produced some

Wiring this tier is therefore a small file, not a project. It differs from the
tier-4 one in exactly two ways:

  1. the adapter it sources
         SEANCE_ADAPTER=lib/adapter.subr
  2. the world the vectors are written against, declared in the environment
     because the vector file names it with placeholders rather than with
     fixture names:
         SEANCE_CONF_JAIL     a jail-type guest that exists on the node
         SEANCE_CONF_VM       a bhyve guest that exists (or omit its rows)
         SEANCE_CONF_HELD     a guest put into slave mode by the stage
         SEANCE_CONF_ABSENT   a name no guest has
         SEANCE_CONF_BADNAME  a name no guest may have (default UPPER)

The vectors are read-only by construction: the mutating verbs appear in them
only with a name their contract requires them to refuse before touching
anything. The lifecycle proper -- create a minimal jail, register, start,
probe, hold, release, stop, unregister -- is a staged test of its own and is
what M2 adds here, together with the two live assertions M0 and M1 left
UNVERIFIED:

  * a real jail in slave mode refuses `cbsd jstart` (decision D-21);
  * `cbsd jstatus jname=<n> invert=1` (what `adapter_guest_running` actually
    calls, D-47) for a guest that EXISTS exits 0 and prints its jid
    (docs/cbsd-module-notes.md §6 has the source-level argument for this and
    marks it UNVERIFIED live until this tier; M0 had no guest to run it
    against).

M2 NOTE -- adapter_guest_datasets now resolves the MOUNTED PATH first.

A guest promoted in place keeps its dataset under <standby_root>/<dead>/<name>
and is given the mountpoint CBSD expects (D-44 item 4), so a derivation from
the pool that carries jails-data can no longer name it. lib/adapter.subr
therefore asks `zfs list -H -o name <mountpoint>` first and falls back to the
pool derivation. Two live assertions belong to this tier and are UNVERIFIED
until it runs:

  * against a real CBSD jail at home, `adapter_guest_datasets <jail>` names the
    same dataset CBSD created (`<pool>/<jname>`, sudoexec/mkdatadir:20-21), and
    against a real bhyve guest it names the one mounted at ${workdir}/vm/<name>
    (sudoexec/bcreate:579) -- resolution 1, on the real thing;
  * with the guest's dataset UNMOUNTED, the same call falls back rather than
    resolving to CBSD's whole workdir dataset. `zfs list <path>` answers with
    the dataset CONTAINING the path (observed on the workstation:
    `zfs list -H -o name,mountpoint /data/jails` prints the parent and /data),
    which is why the adapter checks that the answer's own mountpoint is exactly
    the path asked about. tests/tier4/t_adapter_parse.sh pins this against a
    scripted zfs; this tier is where it meets a real pool.

Also UNVERIFIED here, and it matters more than it looks: a JAIL's registerable
configuration lives at ${jailsysdir}/<name>/rc.conf_<name>, which is NOT inside
the guest's replicated dataset, while a VM's is (jails-system/<name> is a
symlink to ${workdir}/vm/<name>, sudoexec/bcreate:599). See the open problem
noted in lib/promote.subr's header: a promoted VM finds its configuration, and
a promoted jail on real CBSD may not.

Substrate note from M0, binding on this tier: `cbsd initenv` starts cbsdd,
which holds files open under the reset dataset, so a stage here must stop
cbsdd (and cbsdrsyncd) before `reaper reset` -- or keep the CBSD workdir out
of the reset dataset entirely.

Files named t_*.sh are tests; anything else here is ignored.
